{"The metrics tag key is inconsistent between spans.buffer.flusher.produce (uses 'shard') and spans.buffer.flusher.wait_produce (uses 'shards'). This makes it harder to correlate timings for the same shard set.": 0.777237594127655, "time.sleep is monkeypatched to a no-op at the start of the test, so the later time.sleep(0.1) does not actually wait and can make the test flaky. Prefer a deterministic wait or call an unpatched sleep function.": 0.8946835994720459, "The docstring claims this creates 'one process per shard', but the implementation can share shards across processes when max_processes is lower than the shard count. Adjust the wording to reflect the configurable upper bound.": 0.22445446252822876, "self.num_processes can become 0 (e.g. empty buffer.assigned_shards, or max_processes=0 since 'max_processes or ...' treats 0 as falsy). That leads to 'i % self.num_processes' raising ZeroDivisionError during shard mapping. Add validation and avoid using 'or' for the defaulting behavior.": 0.9086565375328064, "Creating a separate SpansBuffer per process means each process will independently apply spans.buffer.max-flush-segments (default 500) per cycle. In aggregate this can increase the total flushed segments per wall-clock cycle by ~num_processes\u00d7 compared to the previous single-flusher behavior, which may have significant Kafka/Redis load implications. Consider splitting the configured flush budget across processes, or updating the option/documentation to reflect the new semantics.": 0.7068265676498413, "With default=1, the new --flusher-processes option forces single-process flushing unless explicitly overridden, which conflicts with the stated goal of spawning per-shard processes. Also, type=int allows 0/negative values that can break flusher initialization. Consider defaulting to None (meaning 'one per shard') and enforcing a minimum of 1 when provided.": 0.7726007699966431}